# Cache-Control for the Eddie runtime on Cloudflare Pages or Netlify: copy
# this file to your site's static/ directory (it is served as /_headers).
#
# Two independent `?v=` values, so a content deploy does not re-download the
# engine:
#
#   the runtime assets (eddie-widget.js, eddie-worker.js, eddie-sw-*.js,
#   eddie-lite.wasm, eddie-dense.wasm and the wasm glue) carry
#   ?v=<asset hash>, stamped into every bundle by widget/build.sh from the
#   files that produced them. It changes when Eddie is upgraded, not when
#   the site is rebuilt.
#
#   index.ed, its index.<lane>.ed sidecars and models/<lane>/* carry
#   ?v=<index hash> from the Hugo partial. Those really are rebuilt together.
#
# Both hosts match rules on the path only; the query string is part of the
# browser's and the CDN's cache key, which is what makes ?v= work with
# `immutable`.
#
# eddie-boot.js is the one URL the page references without a ?v=, so it is
# the one that has to keep revalidating: it carries the asset hash the rest
# of the runtime is fetched with. It is deliberately NOT covered by a
# `/eddie/*` wildcard -- Cloudflare Pages merges the directives of every
# matching rule rather than letting a later rule replace an earlier one, so
# a wildcard that says `immutable` makes eddie-boot.js immutable too and the
# site cannot ship an upgrade until the browser's cache expires. Naming the
# long-lived files explicitly is longer, but it is the version that works.

/eddie/eddie-widget.js
/eddie/eddie-worker.js
/eddie/eddie-agent-worker.js
/eddie/eddie-sw-lite.js
/eddie/eddie-sw-dense.js
/eddie/eddie-sw-gpu.js
/eddie/eddie-sw-agent.js
/eddie/eddie-transformers-sw.js
/eddie/eddie-lite.js
/eddie/eddie-lite-esm.js
/eddie/eddie-lite.wasm
/eddie/eddie-dense.js
/eddie/eddie-dense-esm.js
/eddie/eddie-dense.wasm
/eddie/index.ed
/eddie/index.*.ed
/eddie/models/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/eddie/eddie-boot.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

# With `loader = "full"` the page references eddie-widget.js directly, again
# without a ?v=. Move it out of the list above and give it the boot script's
# rule instead; it then revalidates on every visit rather than being fetched
# once, which is the trade the boot loader exists to avoid.
